Builds iOS arm64 objects for arm64 only, not arm64 + arm64e.

gn/skia/BUILD.gn gives every iOS arm64 compile "-arch arm64 -arch arm64e", so each
object, and each archive, is a fat file with both slices. That doubles libskia.a
(27 MB for Release against 13 MB for macOS arm64), confuses tools that expect thin
members, and buys nothing for a third-party app: the App Store does not take
arm64e from it. The rust archives that Bazel builds are arm64 only.

Only the iOS arm64 branch changes; macOS and the other CPUs are untouched. The
simulator (ios_use_simulator) takes the same branch and is arm64 only too.

Checked on Skia 157 (b94213a11b) with Xcode 27.2: lipo -info libskia.a says
"arm64" alone, for target_os = "ios" both with and without ios_use_simulator.

diff --git a/gn/skia/BUILD.gn b/gn/skia/BUILD.gn
index 3e4c8fb..89b24c7 100644
--- a/gn/skia/BUILD.gn
+++ b/gn/skia/BUILD.gn
@@ -190,11 +190,11 @@ config("default") {
         "armv7",
       ]
     } else if (current_cpu == "arm64") {
+      # skia-prep: arm64 only. arm64e makes every object (and archive) fat, doubling
+      # the size, and an App Store app cannot ship it; the rust archives are arm64 only.
       _arch_flags = [
         "-arch",
         "arm64",
-        "-arch",
-        "arm64e",
       ]
     } else if (current_cpu == "x86") {
       _arch_flags = [
